ing sense  of that information with a combination  of statistics and computer programming  the professions expanded rapidly in  recent years and comes with an average  salary of $130,000 a year much of the  data Ivana works with comes from  technology the NBA adopted in 2013 every  NBA arena has cameras that record games  and then film those cameras they can  extract player locations in a court  these cameras record 25 frames per  second so for each basketball game  there's a million of rows of data and  that allows Ivana to analyze plays that  were previously difficult to track for  example take the pick-and-roll it's one  of the most important plays in  basketball it involves one player  setting up a human shield to help a  teammate shake off an opponent so we can  look at each player how much they round  pick and rolls and how how good they are  at that and then we can select a player  and see how often he's gonna pass out  with the pick-and-roll help and he's  gonna shoot and how would that help you  develop a strategy for the coaches also  we could for example this player he's  gonna pass pretty frequently we can say  this to the coaches and they're gonna  decide on the defensive strategy for the  player but this makes it vaana's job  looks simpler than it actually is  because he spends a lot of her time  coding to extract the information she  needs and I tried really hard to get her  to talk about what exactly she looks for  and there's some stuff that you do  that's beyond just these interfaces that  you showed me today right yeah can you  tell me more about that  not really so that's that's like a trade  secret  yes MBAs are very competitive League so  whatever can give us that advantage  which other people when advising the  coaches with her analysis Ivana has a  decided advantage over many of her peers  I started playing when I was 7 years old  and I just loved it from the first day I  think because the game is so dynamic and  there's so many different skills like  what I wanted to do since I was 7 years  old really I wanted to be a professional  basketball player at 19 Ivana moved from  her home in Croatia to New Jersey to go  to college and a basketball scholarship  and while she was a star player on her  Division one team she was an exceptional  math student much of her life has been  like that balancing her love of  basketball with their love of math like  I almost thought I'm gonna have to  choose between the two I went to  graduate school and that's when I really  thought that okay this is really I'm  really choosing one or the other at this  time and so I I thought I really chose  just math the plan was to become a  researcher or a professor then three  years into pursuing her PhD in math she  heard that NBA teams were starting to  hire data scientists but only 26 percent  of data scientists are women and in  vanna didn't like her odds when I saw  the job posting I didn't think I would  actually get the job I thought because  being a woman in such a male-dominated  field I would they would never really  consider me give me a fair shot but this  prediction  turned out to be wrong she's got both  the technical and ocelot backgrounds  which is sort of the ideal mix her  ability to kind of capture these complex  insights and then share them with  players with coaches with executives in  a way that makes sense to us is super  valuable and frankly not that common the  next evening I attended my very first  NBA game it was the last game of the  regular season before the playoffs and  my chance to catch up with the ultimate  beneficiary of Ivana's work  the Sixers head coach Brett Brown  the team has had a great season so far  what role do you think analytics is  played in that's the best I mean I think  it's played a significant role in our  success and many many others the NBA  playoffs are gonna start in three or  four days and immediately we'll get sort  of an analytical assessment on the  strengths or weaknesses 